\begin{abstract}
Research on multimodal emotion recognition in conversation often reuses pre-extracted
utterance features. We audit the widely reused IEMOCAP features released with bc-LSTM. The
original study reports supervised extraction of their text and visual features on the
standard training split. Although the exact configuration of the released six-class file
is undocumented, its session-wise behaviour is consistent with fitting on sessions 1--4,
which leave-one-session-out (LOSO), leave-one-speaker-out and random cross-validation use
as test data. With one fixed model, holding out session 4 instead of session 5 raises
text-only accuracy by 31 points (95\% dialogue-bootstrap interval $[+26.0, +36.6]$); with three
frozen text encoders never trained on IEMOCAP labels it is between $-6.9$ and $-8.0$ points,
and its sign holds when training-set size and representation width are matched. Under
five-fold LOSO the released text features reach 83.5\% accuracy against 59.5\% on the
standard split, while frozen encoders show no inflation. The released visual block gives
suggestive evidence of the same pattern. In a purposive code audit of 20 systems, five
load the shared text block, all evaluated on the standard split, and we found no
published result affected. We recommend the standard split for these files, a session-gap
check before cross-validating on shared features, and frozen encoders when
cross-validation is required.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
emotion recognition in conversation, data leakage, evaluation protocol, IEMOCAP,
multimodal features, reproducibility
