%------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Introduction}

Multimodal emotion recognition in conversation (MERC) has converged on a small set of
corpora and, for IEMOCAP~\cite{busso2008}, on a small set of pre-extracted feature files.
Most systems load utterance-level vectors distributed with earlier
work~\cite{poria2017,ghosal2019,hu2021mmgcn} instead of processing raw audio and video.
The practice lowers the cost of entry and keeps systems comparable, and it makes the
provenance of those files part of every result built on them.

Among the most reused IEMOCAP features are those introduced with
bc-LSTM~\cite{poria2017}. The original study reports that their text and visual vectors
are hidden activations of networks trained on the emotion labels of the training split,
sessions 1--4, and that the audio vectors are unsupervised acoustic descriptors. This does
not affect the standard split, which tests on session 5. IEMOCAP, however, has no agreed
test set: papers also report leave-one-session-out and speaker-independent
cross-validation, and a recent audit recommends cross-validation~\cite{antoniou2023}. If
the released file was produced as reported, four of the five test folds of these
protocols are sessions the feature extractors were fitted to. We test the hypothesis that
the released features then carry test labels into any protocol that tests on sessions
1--4. The test needs only the feature files: a block without this leak should show no
advantage on session 4 over session 5.

Pre-extracted files are the usual entry point to MERC for groups that do not process raw
recordings, so their validity limits that of the comparisons built on them. In a
purposive code audit of twenty recent systems, five load the shared text block
(Section~\ref{sec:prov}). A leak of this kind does not show in a results table and appears
only when the protocol changes.

In this paper, we audit these features. Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
\item a session-gap measurement per feature block, with frozen text encoders as a
  label-free control, a matched design for training-set size and representation width,
  and a training-free nearest-neighbour check (Section~\ref{sec:gap});
\item a measurement of leave-one-session-out inflation with a bi-LSTM, a linear probe and
  DialogueCRN, together with leave-one-speaker-out and random cross-validation
  (Section~\ref{sec:loso});
\item a comparison of the released feature files and a code audit of their use
  (Section~\ref{sec:prov});
\item an analysis of protocol-dependent rankings and of a fusion comparison on the
  standard split (Section~\ref{sec:conseq});
\item a session-gap diagnostic that needs only the feature files
  (Section~\ref{sec:rec}).
\end{itemize}
We do not claim that any published standard-split result is wrong.

