\section{Related Work}

\textbf{Data leakage.} Information from the evaluation data reaching the model during
training is a recognised cause of irreproducible results across machine-learning-based
science~\cite{kapoor2023}. In neuroimaging, leakage through feature selection has been
shown to inflate prediction performance substantially~\cite{rosenblatt2024}, and in NLP
the analogous concern is benchmark contamination of large language
models~\cite{balloccu2024}. A common form is preprocessing fitted on the full dataset
before splitting; pre-extracted features trained on a fixed split and later reused under
a different split are a variant of it that the user of the files cannot see.

\textbf{IEMOCAP evaluation.} IEMOCAP lacks an agreed test set, and papers report
speaker-dependent splits, session-based splits and five- or ten-fold cross-validation,
often with different label sets~\cite{antoniou2023}. Recent toolkits ship fixed
partitions to counter inconsistent splits~\cite{ma2024emobox}, and a recent critique of
MERC evaluation identifies inconsistent feature sets, tuning on the test set and seed
selection as sources of unreliable comparisons~\cite{tu2026}. These works do not address
how the shared features were produced.

\textbf{Feature reuse in MERC.} Pre-extracted features have become the common substrate of
conversational models: the bc-LSTM features~\cite{poria2017} are loaded, by code
inspection, by the DialogueRNN and DialogueGCN releases~\cite{majumder2019,ghosal2019},
and MMGCN~\cite{hu2021mmgcn} distributes a file that later models reuse in turn; a recent
survey reviews the families of MERC models built this way and how they are
evaluated~\cite{wu2025merc}. These releases document how the features were extracted but
leave open which evaluation protocols the extraction makes unsafe. The one cross-validated
evaluation we found with the same extraction pipeline~\cite{poria2018baselines} states
neither whether it used these files nor whether the extractors were retrained per fold.
We connect provenance and protocol and measure the effect.

