\section{Experimental Setup}\label{sec:setup}

\textbf{Data.} IEMOCAP~\cite{busso2008} contains 151 dyadic dialogues in five sessions,
each session performed by a different pair of actors. The released feature file covers
7{,}433 utterances with six emotion labels (happy, sad, neutral, angry, excited,
frustrated), split into 120 training dialogues (sessions 1--4) and 31 test dialogues
(session 5). It provides 100-d text, 100-d audio and 512-d visual vectors per utterance.

\textbf{How the released features were built.} Poria et al.~\cite{poria2017} describe a
text network that applies convolutions to word2vec embeddings and a visual 3D
convolutional network, each topped by a fully connected layer and a softmax over the
emotion labels and trained on the training split; the activations of the fully connected
layer serve as the utterance features. The audio vectors are openSMILE acoustic
descriptors, computed without labels. The original study thus reports supervised
extraction on the standard training split. The exact configuration of the released
six-class file is undocumented: its text and visual vectors have 100 and 512 dimensions,
and the paper reports four classes. Its session-wise behaviour, measured below, is
consistent with fitting on sessions 1--4. After the reported extraction, we call the text
and visual blocks \emph{supervised} and the audio block \emph{unsupervised}; the
diagnostics test whether a block behaves as if it had seen the labels of the sessions it
is evaluated on.

\textbf{Frozen encoders.} To obtain text features that were never trained on IEMOCAP
labels, we encoded the transcripts shipped in the same file with BERT-base~\cite{devlin2019},
RoBERTa-base~\cite{liu2019roberta} and DeBERTa-v3-base~\cite{he2023debertav3}, frozen and
never fine-tuned, taking the mean of the last-layer states (half precision, one 6\,GB GPU).

\textbf{Model.} Unless stated otherwise, the trained experiments use one model: a
bidirectional LSTM (bi-LSTM)~\cite{hochreiter1997} over the utterances of a dialogue with
a per-utterance linear read-out (hidden size 100, dropout 0.5, Adam with learning rate
$10^{-3}$, up to 50 epochs with early stopping on a seeded 20\% of the training
dialogues), trained with three seeds (five for the fusion experiment of
Section~\ref{sec:conseq}). Only the input features change between runs. Standardisation
and the PCA projection of Section~\ref{sec:gap} are fitted on the training fold only; the
training-free nearest-neighbour check standardises over all utterances without using
labels.

\textbf{Protocols.} \emph{Standard}: train on sessions 1--4, test on session 5.
\emph{Session-4 hold-out}: train on sessions 1--3, test on session 4; both test sets are
unseen by the model, but only session 5 is unseen by the feature extractors.
\emph{Leave-one-session-out (LOSO)}: five folds, each session tested once by a model
trained on the other four; fold 5 equals the standard split, and the LOSO mean is the
unweighted mean over folds. \emph{Leave-one-speaker-out}: ten folds, one actor held out
per fold, read by the linear probe because a dialogue contains two speakers.
\emph{Random five-fold}: folds drawn over dialogues, ignoring sessions, so every test
fold mixes sessions 1--4 with session 5.

\textbf{Uncertainty.} Seeds vary only the initialisation. For test-set uncertainty the
sampling unit is the dialogue, since the utterances of a dialogue share speakers and
context: 95\% intervals are percentiles of 10{,}000 resamples of the test dialogues,
drawn with replacement within each held-out session, with per-dialogue correct counts
averaged over seeds. Results per fold and per seed are in the accompanying repository.

