Table~\ref{tab:gap} shows the result. The released text features give 90.61\% on session 4
and 59.54\% on session 5, and unsupervised audio moves by six points. With each of the
three frozen encoders the gap is negative, between $-6.9$ and $-8.0$ points; on these
features session 4 is the harder session, which can happen for two sessions performed by
different actors. The frozen encoders also reach 2.2 to 5.1 points higher accuracy than
the released text features on the standard split.

\textbf{Label shift.} Sessions differ in class balance: the largest class covers 31.8\% of
session 4 and 23.7\% of session 5, and the per-session counts are in the accompanying
repository. An 8-point difference in majority-class share is unlikely to explain the gap
of the released text, and the frozen encoders, which score \emph{lower} on session 4, show
that the class balance does not favour session 4 by itself. A pretrained language model
may have encountered the scripted plays that part of IEMOCAP performs, but not their
emotion labels, which are what this test concerns.

\textbf{Nearest-neighbour check.} This check trains no model: for every utterance, take
its ten nearest neighbours (cosine similarity on standardised vectors) among the
utterances of the same session but of other dialogues, and predict their majority label.
An extractor fitted to the labels of sessions 1--4 should have placed those sessions'
utterances in label-pure neighbourhoods, and session 5's less so. Fig.~\ref{fig:knn}
shows this pattern for the released text block: neighbours agree with the label for
89.6\% of the utterances of sessions 1--4 on average and for 47.5\% of those of session
5, a drop of 42 points. With 1, 5, 10 and 20 neighbours the drop is $+41.3$, $+41.6$,
$+42.1$ and $+40.3$ points, and between $+0.3$ and $+2.3$ for frozen BERT. With ten
neighbours the frozen text encoders give between $+0.6$ and $+1.5$ points, MMGCN's audio
block $+1.4$ and MMGCN's visual block $-5.1$. The check does not separate the released
visual block ($+8.3$) from the released audio ($+5.9$); the two differ only once a model
is trained on them (Table~\ref{tab:gap}). The released audio block, described as
unsupervised, drops by about as much as its session gap ($+5.9$); the file does not show
whether this reflects session difficulty or an undocumented preparation step.

