\textbf{Training-set size and representation width.} Two differences other than label
fitting could produce Table~\ref{tab:gap}. Session 4 is tested by a model trained on three
sessions and session 5 by one trained on four, and the released text block has 100
dimensions where the frozen encoders have 768. We therefore trained every model on exactly
three sessions, averaging over the four such subsets for each held-out session, and
compared the mean accuracy on held-out sessions 1--4 with that on session 5. The released
text is $+30.2$ points higher on sessions 1--4 ($+31.2$ macro F1, $+32.2$ unweighted
average recall) and the released visual block $+19.0$, while frozen BERT is $-3.9$.
Under the same design, projecting the frozen encoders onto 100 dimensions with a PCA
fitted on the training sessions gives $-4.9$ for BERT and $-6.0$ for RoBERTa. In both
controls the sign of each gap is unchanged.

