\section{Leave-One-Session-Out Inflation}\label{sec:loso}

Table~\ref{tab:loso} and Fig.~\ref{fig:loso} report results under the protocol that the
suspected leak affects. With the released text features, folds 1--4 score between 88.1\%
and 91.9\%, fold 5 scores 59.54\%, and the five-fold mean is 83.51\%. Fusing all three
released blocks, the configuration most papers report, gives a five-fold mean of 83.71\%
against 57.24\%. The visual block alone inflates by 16 points; we could not replace it
with a clean encoder, so this inflation and its session gap are the only evidence on it.
Dialogue-bootstrap 95\% intervals for the inflation are $[+20.1, +28.1]$ points for the released text, $[+22.9, +30.0]$ for the three released blocks fused, $[+11.2, +21.6]$ for the visual block and $[-2.5, +8.8]$ for audio; for the frozen text encoders they span $-9.7$ to $+0.5$. Unsupervised audio moves by three points. With frozen text encoders the five-fold
mean is \emph{below} the standard split by 3.8 to 5.2 points (1.4 with BERT text plus
released audio), consistent with session 5 being the easier test set for these features.
Because weighted F1 can hide a shift towards frequent classes, we also record macro F1,
which moves with accuracy in every row: $+24.9$ for the released text, $+14.4$ for the
visual block, $+2.7$ for audio and $+26.5$ for the three released blocks fused, against
$-3.4$ to $-5.6$ for the frozen text encoders ($-2.0$ with the released audio added).

