\textbf{Stress test with a published architecture.} As a leakage stress test, not a
replication, we trained DialogueCRN~\cite{hu2021dialoguecrn}, one of the systems that load
the shared text block, with its released model, reasoning modules, loss and
hyper-parameter values under the same five folds and three seeds. With the released text
block it scores between 87.98 and 90.81 on the folds from sessions 1--4 (87.3 to 91.7
across individual seeds) and 57.84 on session 5, a five-fold mean of 83.07 ($+25.2$
points of accuracy, $+25.1$ of weighted F1). With frozen BERT text in the same code the
five-fold mean is 59.65 against 61.72 ($-2.1$ points). Seed standard deviations are at
most 2.6 points. The run departs from the released code in its session-based folds,
where the code hard-codes session 5; in standardising the input block on each training
fold, without which the 768-dimensional encoder states barely train and which moves the
released-text result by 0.1 points; in selecting the epoch on 10\% of the training
dialogues, where the code selects on the test fold; and in using our own training loop
around the released model. The folds, standardisation and epoch selection apply to both
feature blocks. With the default reasoning steps and no search over them, the setup
reaches about 58 accuracy on the standard split, below the 66.05 the repository reports,
so these numbers compare the two feature blocks with each other and not with the
published figure. The commit, environment, seeds and differences are listed in the
accompanying repository.

