\section{Feature Provenance and Usage}\label{sec:prov}

We compared, utterance by utterance, the bc-LSTM release with the
\texttt{IEMOCAP\_features.pkl} files shipped by the DialogueGCN and MMGCN code releases
(Fig.~\ref{fig:prov}). The DialogueGCN file is identical to the bc-LSTM file in every
block. The MMGCN file keeps the identical text block and replaces the audio and visual
blocks with a 1{,}582-d acoustic descriptor and a 342-d visual descriptor. Utterance ids,
labels and the train/test split are the same in all three. The shared text block, and
with it the suspected leak, can therefore reach any model that reads the text block of
any of the three files. MMGCN's own audio and visual blocks show no sign of it: under LOSO
their five-fold means are slightly \emph{below} the standard split (audio 48.78\% against
50.56\%, visual 34.82\% against 35.10\%), and their session gaps are negative ($g=-2.5$
points for audio and $-6.7$ for visual), while the shared text block in the same file
gives $+31.1$.

