\section{Downstream Effects}\label{sec:conseq}

\textbf{Fusion with released blocks.} On the standard split, adding the released audio
and visual blocks to frozen BERT text lowers accuracy by 3.2 points without
conversational context and by 6.6 points with it (five seeds). Taken alone, this could
suggest that extra modalities hurt once context is available. Adding the blocks
separately gives a different picture: audio alone changes accuracy by $+1.6$ and $+0.4$
points, and the visual block alone lowers it by 6.6 and 8.9 (Table~\ref{tab:trap}; paired
$t$-test $p\le3\times10^{-4}$ for the visual block). The loss is consistent with poor
transfer of the released visual block to session 5; this experiment does not establish
its cause.

