\section{Discussion}

\textbf{Origin of the hazard.} The original extraction suited its purpose: a fixed split,
extractors trained on its training part, and features released for replication. The
hazard arose later. Releases that did not restate the provenance reused the files as
generic features, and evaluation practice moved towards cross-validation. Neither change
shows in a results table, and the provenance is written only in the original paper. In
the terms of Kapoor and Narayanan~\cite{kapoor2023}, the pattern we measure matches
leakage from preprocessing fitted on data that later serves as test data, here
distributed as a file.

\textbf{Scope of the claims.} This leak does not affect standard-split results on these
files, and our audit found no published cross-validated result on them. The claim is
therefore a hazard and its measured size. The LOSO inflation of Section~\ref{sec:loso} is
larger than the one- or two-point margins on which new systems are often compared.

\textbf{Cost of re-extraction.} The frozen text encoders run inference-only on a 6\,GB
consumer GPU and give higher standard-split accuracy than the released text features
(Section~\ref{sec:gap}). Re-extracting video needs the raw recordings and costs more.

\textbf{Provenance metadata.} A feature release can state which utterances its extractors
were fitted to. We recommend this field for shared feature files, in the spirit of
datasheets for datasets~\cite{gebru2021datasheets}.

%------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Recommendations}\label{sec:rec}

The following practices complement general checklists such as
REFORMS~\cite{kapoor2024reforms}.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{State the protocol and use the standard split.} On these files the test data
  should be unseen by the feature extractors: train on sessions 1--4, test on session 5.
  With features re-extracted inside each fold, or with encoders that never saw the
  labels, leave-one-session-out remains a sound protocol.
\item \textbf{Run the session-gap diagnostic} on a pre-extracted feature block before
  cross-validating (Table~\ref{tab:diag}). A gap well above that of an unsupervised block
  is consistent with extraction fitted to the tested sessions. With a linear probe on the
  7{,}433 utterances of this file, fitting both models for one block took 0.2 to 4.3\,s on a desktop CPU (Intel Core i7-12700). The nearest-neighbour check of
  Fig.~\ref{fig:knn} needs no training and can serve as a first screen, but here it did
  not separate the visual block from unsupervised audio, so a small drop does not clear a
  block.
\item \textbf{Re-extract with frozen encoders} when cross-validation is required. Feature
  releases should state which utterances each extractor was fitted to, and reviewers can
  ask for this information when a paper cross-validates on shared features.
\end{enumerate}

