%------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Limitations}

We re-extracted only the text channel of IEMOCAP; re-extracting video requires the raw
recordings. For the visual block the evidence is suggestive only: its session gap and
inflation, with no clean replacement. We tested two trained readers, a bi-LSTM and a
linear probe, one published architecture and one training-free check, which did not
separate the visual block from unsupervised audio; models that rely less on the text
block may be affected less. The rank correlation of Fig.~\ref{fig:rank} rests on eleven
configurations and is not significant on its own. Which label set trained the released
extractors (four or six classes) is not documented, which limits how exactly the leak can
be characterised. The usage survey is a purposive sample of twenty systems, and the
dialogue-bootstrap intervals treat the seeds as fixed.

%------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Conclusion}

The text block of widely reused IEMOCAP features behaves as if fitted to the labels of
four of its five sessions, and the visual block shows suggestive evidence of the same.
Supervised extraction on the training split, as the original study reports, suits the
standard split and exposes other protocols to leakage on these files. Under LOSO the
resulting inflation is large for our readers and for DialogueCRN, and it does not show in
a results table. A session-gap check with a linear probe needs only the feature files and
a CPU, and we recommend it before cross-validating on shared features.

